import{_ as t,o as r,c as o,a2 as i}from"./chunks/framework.DPTp4bo7.js";const h=JSON.parse('{"title":"Some Temporary Ideas","description":"","frontmatter":{"head":[["link",{"rel":"canonical","href":"https://kokobird28144.github.io/endfield-skydoc/some-temporary-ideas"}],["link",{"rel":"alternate","hreflang":"en","href":"https://kokobird28144.github.io/endfield-skydoc/some-temporary-ideas"}],["link",{"rel":"alternate","hreflang":"zh-CN","href":"https://kokobird28144.github.io/endfield-skydoc/zh/some-temporary-ideas"}],["link",{"rel":"alternate","hreflang":"x-default","href":"https://kokobird28144.github.io/endfield-skydoc/some-temporary-ideas"}]]},"headers":[],"relativePath":"some-temporary-ideas.md","filePath":"logistics/src/page-wLO7awzBtdEXp0dRFq54zN.md"}'),n={name:"some-temporary-ideas.md"};function s(a,e,c,p,l,d){return r(),o("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[i('<div class="logistics-content"><span class="logistics-source-anchor" id="一些临时的想法" aria-hidden="true"></span><h1 id="some-temporary-ideas" tabindex="-1">Some Temporary Ideas <a class="header-anchor" href="#some-temporary-ideas" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Some Temporary Ideas&quot;">​</a></h1><blockquote><p>Source page ID: <code>wLO7awzBtdEXp0dRFq54zN</code> ｜ <a href="https://docs.qq.com/aio/DTmluZ1diWlpQZldw?p=wLO7awzBtdEXp0dRFq54zN" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Open in Tencent Docs</a></p></blockquote><p>Assume every component only outputs during computation. A non-Splitter encountering a downstream Converger should activate that Converger&#39;s other non-Converger upstream components, using the Converger&#39;s round-robin counter to determine which successfully inputs.</p><p>During Protocol Stash priority output, the Converger-output branch can accept round-robin input. To reproduce priority for the “earlier upstream connection,” suppose computing a Converger&#39;s upstream component triggers that rotation, making stash output (treated as non-Splitter) rotate with other Converger inputs. Normally, a stash outputs after the entire logistics system; during computation its downstream layer order adds entries to a special structure resembling terminal order, but treating Convergers specially, to enable rotation. This would explain it. Unloader/crucible outputs use wholly different logic and must wait until the whole system finishes, avoiding this round-robin effect.</p><p>What happens when a standard factory tries seamless recipe switching? Consider the symmetry between same-recipe continuity/different-recipe 1tick stalls, and same-product seamless reaction/different-product waiting for previous output. Perhaps failed product output passes the start-recipe stage, skipping the next recipe&#39;s intended same-frame start; output succeeds next tick, then production starts. This suggests (factory input–logistics system)–factory output–recipe completion–recipe start. It is a reasonable solution, but changing logistics would not alter the stall: output cannot move between completion and start. Serial mixed production remains affected and cannot fully resolve it; longer switching cycles only mitigate. Other explanations exist, but I cannot determine whether they give identical results. New experiments show that when reaction products are full and external input and products compete for inventory slots, outputting one drop lets the reaction finish: output precedes recipe completion in the same tick, while input waits until next tick. However, switching multiple recipes each having only one input does not stall 1tick, a counterexample.</p><p><img src="/endfield-skydoc/logistics/src/assets/images/img-4d8752ebec3d70cb.q90-f8b22f1246ad9ef2.webp" alt="Some Temporary Ideas"></p><p>0tick: a enters, a reacts; 8tick: b enters, A completes, b reacts; 9tick: A outputs; 16tick: a enters, b completes, a reacts. Seems fine again.</p><p>Now crucible multi-product recipes&#39; products also prioritize output. What the hell.</p><p>Another explanation: after output, a Converger polls upstream to pull; Splitters cannot be pulled from, keeping crucible/Unloader output last. This necessarily permits pulling stash contents. Can we construct a case pulling an input that has not yet entered, causing a 1tick stall? Consider <a href="/endfield-skydoc/introduction-to-analyzing-endfield-logistics-bugs">Introduction to Analyzing Endfield Logistics Bugs</a> when Splitter and Converger share a layer. If the Converger computes first, it outputs and pulls from the upstream belt, not the Splitter (which cannot be pulled). If the Splitter computes first, the Converger has not output, so splitting into it is impossible. Splitting would always fail, contradicting reality; matching observation therefore requires extra within-layer ordering changes. This could introduce an entirely different system, so leave the thought here for now.</p><p>Another solution: direct Splitter–Converger connection forces a particular priority. Additionally, Convergers use a pull mechanism. This has rather high freedom: priority assignment and computation order can both be claimed to change for various reasons. I currently cannot verify it.</p><p>Earlier crucible discussion hypothesized a void buffer: products enter it first, then real storage, and the reaction is considered unfinished until real storage accepts them. This cannot be verified.</p><p>PL thinks whether crucible products prioritize output or reaction depends on recipe traversal order, apparently.</p></div>',1)])])}const m=t(n,[["render",s]]);export{h as __pageData,m as default};
